\begin{document}
\begin{frontmatter}
\title{Identification Limits and Resolution-Aware Release for Latent-State Measurement with Imperfect Proxies}
\runtitle{Identification Limits for Imperfect Proxy Measurement}

\begin{aug}
\author[A]{\fnms{Mahsa}~\snm{Keikha}\ead[label=e1]{mahsa@connectioncare.net}}
\address[A]{Connected Care LLC, California, USA\printead[presep={,\ }]{e1}}
\runauthor{M. Keikha}
\end{aug}

\begin{abstract}
Many scientific targets are latent and are inferred through imperfect proxies. Even when sensitivity and specificity are available, inverse correction can be poorly identified, unstable, or too imprecise for the intended claim. We develop an identification-first framework for latent-state measurement with imperfect binary proxies. The framework propagates finite calibration uncertainty, gives sharp bounds under unrestricted missing outcomes, proves an exact missingness-information law, quantifies inverse instability through the Youden information margin, and derives a finite-sample resolution law. For multisite studies, we prove that a pooled proxy rate identifies population-average latent prevalence for every feasible site composition if and only if site information margins are equal; otherwise we give a sharp closed-form identified set. Finally, we distinguish same-data abstention from a selection-safe design in which an independent pilot gate is separated from confirmatory inference. Deterministic experiments reproduce the analytic regimes and expose their engineering consequences. The motivating application is consciousness measurement, but the results apply to any latent-state system built on fallible proxy channels.
\end{abstract}

